    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Claims now show their due-by date and whether they're overdue, and each person and the sheet as a whole show an overdue count and overdue total. The acceptance test passes under `bin/e2e`; I haven't checked anything beyond that one test.\n\n**Server**\n- `terms` is a new key in the existing settings table, so no database migration was needed.\n- It defaults to 14 days. The settings form accepts a blank value (meaning \"leave it as it is\") or a whole number of days from 0 up to 4 digits; anything else is refused with `settings-error`.\n- Changing it writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=terms value=<days>` to the audit file. Saving the same value again writes nothing, the same as the other settings.\n\n**Front end**\n- The settings panel has a terms field (`setting-terms`) with the value in force shown beside it (`setting-terms-current`).\n- Each claim row shows `claim-due-by` (the claim's date plus the terms) and `claim-overdue` (`yes` when the claim is OPEN and its due-by is before the sheet's today).\n- The new file `features/claims/overdue.slot.tsx` adds two columns to each person's row (`person-overdue-count` and `person-overdue-total`) and adds the sheet totals (`rollup-overdue-count` and `rollup-overdue-total`).\n\nThree behaviours to be aware of:\n- **Held people:** the counts and totals use the same rule as the other totals, so the OPEN claims of a person on hold are left out. Those claims can still show `overdue: yes` on their own rows.\n- **No rate:** an overdue claim whose due shows n/a still adds 1 to the count but adds 0 to the overdue total.\n- **Terms apply backwards:** due-by always uses the current terms, so changing them moves the due-by of every existing claim too.",
